schemaVersion: "2"
kind: mixin
name: yoki-codex
requires:
  agent: codex
displayName: yoki harness for Codex
description: >-
  Fills the gaps in the sandbox base image and wires ~/.codex to the same
  claude-profiles layers (core + packs + personal) the host uses, so a
  sandboxed Codex session gets the same hooks/rules/agents/skills parity
  yoki-claude already has (task T15 closes the gap yoki-claude's kit left
  open — see yoki-omp's kit for the same shape a generation earlier).

# Rendered by yoki-box at launch, into a temp dir, and never installed as a
# file — same reasoning as the claude kit's spec.yaml.in: {{DOTFILES_ROOT}}
# is a literal path belonging to one checkout, and a sandbox mounts the repo
# at its HOST path, so a generated artifact would be wrong the moment
# yoki-box runs from a worktree. The `.in` suffix (not `.template`) keeps
# core/config/manager.sh from generating it. This kit now embeds a host
# path — previously it did not, and was a plain spec.yaml (yoki-box keys the
# read-only dotfiles mounts off spec.yaml.in's presence, so converting it is
# what turns the mounts on below).
#
# Measured on the Codex base image 2026-08-15 (Ubuntu, user `agent`, home
# /home/agent, codex-cli 0.146.0): node, git, jq and rg are PRESENT; zsh,
# mise and fd are MISSING — the same three gaps as the Claude image, so the
# install list is identical.
#
# WHAT THIS KIT WRITES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
#
# 1. `yoki-switch apply --target codex` in startup (below) runs ONLY the
#    codex generator (lib/targets/gen.js via yoki-switch's
#    apply_target_generator) — never the plain `yoki-switch apply` the
#    claude kit runs, which would also attempt the ~/.claude merge_settings
#    step. That step is harmless here (~/.claude does not exist in a Codex
#    sandbox, so `apply`'s claude branch would just run merge_settings
#    against a fresh $CLAUDE_DIR — wasted work, not wrong), but --target
#    codex says precisely what this kit needs and nothing else.
#
# 2. It does NOT overwrite ~/.codex/config.toml wholesale. sbx already
#    writes one at create time ("yolo mode": approval_policy = "never",
#    sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access") and that same file carries the
#    mcp-gateway server with its `Bearer proxy-managed` header — clobbering
#    it would silently cut MCP off. codex-config-toml.js's managed-block
#    writer only ever touches the `# yoki:begin`/`# yoki:end` block it owns,
#    leaving sbx's `sandbox_mode`/`approval_policy` lines and the
#    `[mcp_servers.*]` tables outside it untouched.
#
#    That same top-level `sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access"` line is also
#    why the generator's own conflict rule matters here specifically: Codex
#    rejects `default_permissions` combined with a top-level `sandbox_mode`
#    (spike S3 §1), so codex-config-toml.js's hasConflictingTopLevelKey()
#    detects it ahead of the managed block and skips writing
#    `default_permissions = "yoki"` (and the whole permissions/hooks-state
#    block) entirely, with a warning, rather than writing a config Codex
#    would silently misinterpret. Verified directly (core/validation/
#    test-targets-golden.sh) by running the generator against a config.toml
#    fixture that carries the exact `sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access"`
#    line sbx writes: default_permissions stays absent and sbx's own lines
#    survive byte-for-byte. Net effect: inside this sandbox, Codex keeps
#    running under sbx's own yolo-mode permissions — the yoki execpolicy
#    rules/filesystem-deny table this kit would otherwise install don't
#    apply here, on top of point 3 below.
#
# 3. It therefore leaves Codex more permissive than Claude in the same
#    posture even where the managed block DOES get written. Claude runs
#    `auto` — a classifier reviews every action — because a microVM stops
#    destruction but not disclosure (/run/sandbox/source is readable and the
#    Balanced policy allows 192 domains). Codex has no config-level
#    equivalent of `auto`: `on-request` leaves the decision to the model,
#    which a prompt injection simply declines to use, and `untrusted` asks
#    about everything. The nearest real equivalent is the `--approve-for-me`
#    launch flag (automatic review). Left unwired because sbx's own launch
#    arguments for the codex agent are not introspectable, so overriding
#    them here would be guesswork. Pass it yourself when it matters:
#        ycodex -- --approve-for-me
#    or layer a profile: put yoki.config.toml under $CODEX_HOME and run
#        ycodex -- -p yoki

permissions:
  network:
    allow:
      # apt on arm64 Ubuntu resolves to ports.ubuntu.com, not archive.
      - ports.ubuntu.com
      - archive.ubuntu.com
      - security.ubuntu.com
      # `curl https://mise.run | sh` redirects mise.run -> mise.jdx.dev, and
      # the installer pulls the binary from the GitHub release assets.
      - mise.run
      - mise.jdx.dev
      - github.com
      - objects.githubusercontent.com
      - release-assets.githubusercontent.com

setup:
  install:
    - command: apt-get update && apt-get install -y zsh fd-find
      description: The only base-image gaps besides mise (fd ships as fd-find on Ubuntu)
    - command: curl -fsSL https://mise.run | sh
      user: "1000"
      description: Install mise as the agent user so toolchains land in /home/agent

  startup:
    # yoki-switch is fully portable — CLAUDE_DIR/CODEX_DIR/OMP_AGENT_DIR and
    # DOTFILES_ROOT are all overridable and it contains no uname checks — so
    # it rebuilds ~/.codex here exactly as `apply --target codex` does on
    # the host. `--target codex` restricts it to that one generator (see
    # point 1 above) — never plain `apply`, which the claude kit uses
    # because it needs the ~/.claude merge too.
    - command:
        - sh
        - -c
        - DOTFILES_ROOT={{DOTFILES_ROOT}} bash {{DOTFILES_ROOT}}/domains/dev/bin/yoki-switch apply --target codex
      user: "1000"
      description: Rebuild ~/.codex from core + packs + personal via lib/targets/gen.js
    # Packs are NOT enabled here, same as the claude kit and for the same
    # reason: they default to OFF on a fresh machine and a sandbox is a
    # fresh machine every time. The enabled set is a machine-local choice
    # (~/.claude/.claude-packs on the HOST, not in the repo), so yoki-box's
    # sync_packs() reads it after the sandbox is created and re-runs
    # `yoki-switch pack enable` for exactly that set — see domains/dev/bin/
    # yoki-box.
